= Programming language =

== Overview ==
A '''programming language''' is a notation and rule system for expressing [[Program (computing)|programs]] and computations. It defines which expressions are well formed and what those expressions mean.

Programming languages range from machine-oriented assembly languages to high-level general-purpose languages and narrow domain-specific languages. Their design affects how programmers represent data, control, abstraction, state, concurrency, and interaction.

== Syntax and semantics ==
Two foundational dimensions are:

* '''[[Syntax]]''' — which sequences or structures count as well-formed language expressions;
* '''[[Semantics]]''' — what well-formed expressions mean or how they behave.

A language can have correct syntax while still containing a type error or another semantic violation. Formal language definitions therefore often separate lexical syntax, grammar, static semantics, and dynamic semantics.

== Language and implementation ==
A programming language is not identical to any one compiler, interpreter, runtime, or virtual machine.

One language can have several implementations. Those implementations may differ in optimization, performance, memory management, supported platforms, extensions, and tooling while aiming to preserve the language's specified semantics.

For this reason, labels such as "compiled language" and "interpreted language" can be misleading when treated as intrinsic classifications. Compilation and interpretation are implementation strategies.

== Core language concepts ==
Programming languages commonly provide rules for:

* names and binding;
* scope;
* values and [[Data structure|data structures]];
* types;
* expressions and statements;
* functions/procedures;
* modules and abstraction;
* control flow;
* state and mutation;
* memory and resource management;
* exceptions/effects;
* concurrency or asynchronous behavior;
* interaction with external systems.

Not every language exposes every concept explicitly.

== Type systems ==
A type system classifies expressions or values and restricts which operations are permitted.

Important distinctions include:

* static versus dynamic checking;
* explicit versus inferred types;
* nominal versus structural typing;
* subtyping;
* polymorphism;
* algebraic and compound types;
* type safety and soundness.

Type systems can improve reasoning and tooling, but they differ substantially in expressiveness, guarantees, ergonomics, and runtime behavior.

== Programming paradigms ==
A '''programming paradigm''' is a recurring style for organizing computation. Common labels include:

* imperative;
* procedural;
* object-oriented;
* functional;
* logic;
* declarative;
* dataflow;
* reactive;
* concurrent;
* actor-oriented.

These categories overlap. Many modern languages intentionally support several paradigms.

== Translation and execution ==
A program written in a language may be:

* compiled to native machine code;
* translated to an intermediate representation or bytecode;
* interpreted;
* just-in-time compiled;
* partially evaluated or transformed before/during execution.

The execution strategy belongs to the implementation stack, not necessarily to the language definition itself.

== Formal semantics ==
Programming-language semantics can be described formally.

'''Operational semantics''' describes program behavior in terms of computational steps or transitions.

'''Denotational semantics''' maps language constructs to mathematical meanings.

'''Axiomatic semantics''' characterizes program behavior through logical assertions, such as preconditions and postconditions.

These approaches support reasoning about correctness, equivalence, optimization, and language design.

== Language specification and ecosystem ==
A language may be defined by an international standard, a formal specification, a prose language reference, a reference implementation, or a combination of these.

Libraries, package repositories, build systems, editors, debuggers, and community conventions may be central to practical use while remaining outside the language proper.

== Relationship to computation and programs ==
A programming language is a representational interface between abstract [[Computation|computation]] and executable [[Program (computing)|programs]]. Language constructs make particular computational patterns easier to express, but they do not change the underlying distinction among method, representation, execution, and environment.

== Epistemic notes ==
Programming-language categories are useful but often fuzzy. "Scripting," "systems," "functional," "object-oriented," "compiled," and similar labels can describe dominant usage, implementation choices, or available features rather than exclusive natural kinds.

